However, if your assignment expects you to use an empty string to build the result, you can do that instead:

```java
public static String scrambleWord(String word) {
    String result = "";
    int i = 0;

    while (i < word.length()) {
        if (i + 1 < word.length()
                && word.charAt(i) == 'A'
                && word.charAt(i + 1) != 'A') {

            // Add the two letters in swapped order.
            result += word.charAt(i + 1);
            result += word.charAt(i);

            // Both positions are now finished.
            i += 2;
        } else {
            result += word.charAt(i);
            i++;
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

Here, `word.charAt(i)` gets the character at position `i`, and `result += ...` adds that character to the end of the result string. This version does not need `toCharArray()` because it constructs a new string rather than modifying the original one.

